This feature request addresses the current behavior in the CopilotKit runtime where the `agentId` (used for routing) and the `name` (used for display) are forced to be the same because the runtime uses the object key for both.

<details>

This feature request addresses the current behavior in the CopilotKit runtime where the `agentId` (used for routing) and the `name` (used for display) are forced to be the same because the runtime uses the object key for both.

### 🔍 Analysis of the Current Implementation

In `packages/runtime/src/v2/runtime/handlers/get-runtime-info.ts`, the `/info` endpoint iterates over the resolved agents dictionary:

```typescript
// packages/runtime/src/v2/runtime/handlers/get-runtime-info.ts

const agentEntries = await Promise.all(
  Object.entries(agents).map(async ([name, agent]) => { // 'name' here is the dictionary key (agentId)
    // ...
    const description: AgentDescription = {
      name, // The dictionary key is currently used as the descriptive name
      description: agent.description,
      className: agent.constructor.name,
      ...(capabilities ? { capabilities } : {}),
    };

    return [name, description] as const;
  }),
);
```

As identified in the request, the `name` property in the `AgentDescription` is derived directly from the key in the mapping, making it impossible to have a technical ID (like `customer-support-v1`) separate from a friendly display name (like `Customer Support Agent`).

### 💡 Proposed Solution

To allow `agentId != name`, the runtime should check if the agent instance itself provides a name. Many `AbstractAgent` implementations (from the `@ag-ui/client` protocol used by CopilotKit) support a `name` property.

#### 1. Update `AgentDescription`
Ensure the `AgentDescription` interface in `packages/shared/src/utils/types.ts` clearly represents both if necessary, though `name` is already present and intended for display.

#### 2. Modified Runtime Logic
Update the `handleGetRuntimeInfo` handler to prefer `agent.name` if it exists, falling back to the dictionary key:

```typescript
// packages/runtime/src/v2/runtime/handlers/get-runtime-info.ts

const description: AgentDescription = {
  // Use agent.name if available, otherwise fallback to the dictionary key (id)
  name: (agent as any).name || name, 
  description: agent.description,
  className: agent.constructor.name,
  ...(capabilities ? { capabilities } : {}),
};
```

### 🛠 Example Usage after the change
Developers would then be able to define agents with distinct IDs and names:

```typescript
const runtime = new CopilotRuntime({
  agents: {
    "technical_support_v2": new MyAgent({ 
      name: "Friendly Technical Assistant" 
    })
  }
});
```

The `/info` endpoint would return:
```json
{
  "agents": {
    "technical_support_v2": {
      "name": "Friendly Technical Assistant",
      "description": "...",
      "className": "MyAgent"
    }
  }
}
```

### 🔗 References
- [Runtime Info Handler Source](https://github.com/CopilotKit/CopilotKit/tree/main/CopilotKit/packages/runtime/src/v2/runtime/handlers/get-runtime-info.ts)
- [AgentDescription Type definition](https://github.com/CopilotKit/CopilotKit/tree/main/CopilotKit/packages/shared/src/utils/types.ts)

**Confidence Assessment**: High. The proposed change is a non-breaking enhancement that aligns with how the underlying AG-UI protocol handles agent metadata.

**Would you like to work on this?**
Since you've identified the exact lines of code, this is a great first contribution! If you'd like to proceed, you'll need to update `packages/runtime/src/v2/runtime/handlers/get-runtime-info.ts` and potentially add a test case in `packages/runtime/src/v2/runtime/__tests__/get-runtime-info.test.ts`.

</details>

